#* {{quote-book|en|year=1795|author=[[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]]|title=The Second Part of King Henry the Fourth|section=Act IV, scene IV|lines=41-48|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1518/pg1518-images.html
|text=Learn this, Thomas,<br>And thou shalt prove a shelter to thy friends,<br>A hoop of gold to bind thy brothers in,<br>That the '''united''' vessel of their blood,<br>Mingled with venom of suggestion—<br>As, force perforce, the age will pour it in—<br>Shall never leak [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1823|author=John Marshall|title=Royal Naval Biography|vol=Volume I, Part I|section=Vice-Admirals of the Red|chapter=Gould, Davidge|page=340|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Royal_Naval_Biography/Gould,_Davidge|pageurl=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Royal_Naval_Biography_Marshall_v1p1.djvu/370
|text=The action ended in the capture of the Ca Ira and Censeur, the latter a 74-gun ship, and both crowded with troops. Their '''united''' loss was between 300 and 400 men. On board the British squadron 73 were killed and 275 wounded; the Neapolitans had only 1 slain and 9 wounded.}}
# [[involve|Involving]] the [[joint]] [[activity]] of [[multiple]] [[agent]]s.
#: {{ant|en|disunited|nonunited|ununited}}
#: {{ux|en|The two parties stood '''united''' against the proposal.}}
#: {{ux|en|A '''united''' effort is needed to solve the problem.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1821|author=Joseph Jewell|title=An address to Christians, particularly those who are united in ciruclating the Scriptures|page=6|pageurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/An_address_to_Christians_particularly_th/RcIHAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22are+united%22&pg=PA6&printsec=frontcover
|text=[...] then, I believe, help would be sent from the sanctuary, and we should be strengthened out of Zion to break down the partition walls between us, and no more would be heard of those harsh sentences one against another, founded on far-fetched arguments, and on speculative ideas respecting certain passages contained in those Scriptures of truth, which are now so far '''united''' as to join in sending far and near.<br>Again, I say, I rejoice at this union; but I am sorry to find that we should have any thing like war remaining among us, while we are '''united''' in publishing what we wish to produce &ldquo;peace on earth, and good-will towards men.&rdquo;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1934|title=State of the Union Act|publisher=[[w:Parliament of South Africa|Parliament of South Africa]]|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Status_of_the_Union_Act,_1934
|text=[...] defining the group of self-governing communities composed of Great Britain and the Dominions as “autonomous communities within the British Empire, equal in status, in no way subordinate one to another in any aspect of their domestic or external afiairs, though '''united''' by a common allegiance to the Crown and freely associated as members of the British Commonwealth of Nations” [...]}}

